BLOCKING THE REBUILD
  [2026-09-10] Current-version scoping is in
  ~/docking/scratch/claude_plan_notes/db-preprocessing-plan.md: this rebuild uses
  prepwizard_BioLiP2 after a text-level repair pass (chain-ID + modres fixes below);
  waters/ions, the 20 A trim and the orphan-N filter wait for the next parent DB (no
  s01/s02 rerun now). Items below are unchanged as the record for that rerun.
- Preprocessed DB has zero waters/monatomic ions (checked HOH/ZN/MG/CA/NA/CL/MN/FE
  across all 65,600 files). Not caused by s01 or `_prep_filters`; s02/prepwizard
  behavior is assumed, not verified. Leading suspect: `consolidated_BioLiP2_split`
  splits metals into separate ligand entries. Verify against a source file before
  assuming a fix. This corrupts protonation states everywhere and means
  metal-coordination vdGs can't exist in the current library. Raising trim radius
  does not fix this.
- Trim radius raised 10->20 A in `s01_trim_database.py` (needed for 3rd-shell
  protonation context). Before running: fix stale `origin_dir`/`target_dir` paths,
  and set `overwrite_pdbs = True` (or use a fresh `target_dir`) or the rebuild
  silently writes nothing.
- Fragmenter (`fragment_database_ligs.py`): `mp.Pool`/`imap` hangs forever on a
  worker crash (switch to `ProcessPoolExecutor`); output pickle isn't written
  atomically (write to `.tmp` + `os.replace`); qsub script aborts if the old
  pickle already exists at the output path; an exception mid-run kills the job
  before stats are logged.
- Whole-ring fragments get split across multiple keys (one per cyclic rotation of
  the cut path), fragmenting stats/mining across duplicate libraries. Fix:
  canonicalize whole-ring keys over rotation+reversal before insertion. Doesn't
  apply to aromatic rings.
- prepwizard orphan-backbone-N artifact not caught by `_prep_filters.py` (~461
  dropped environments in the 2026-09-06 run). Needs a filter before rebuild.
- FIXED IN CODE, NOT YET IN DATA: two-character chain IDs written across PDB
  columns 21-22 (`ASPA4  66` = chain A4) collide with the single column every
  reader uses (ProDy, probe, vdG-miner line hashing and ligand scan). 670
  structures in prepwizard_BioLiP2 have such chains (103 with a real residue
  merge; `scratch/chain_fix/twochar_chain_pdbs.txt`), and every one of them was
  dropped whole by the 2026-09-06 build: 0 of 36,853,336 rows (probe and PDB
  lines hash differently -> `find_neighbors` sentinel -> structure skipped).
  Verified on 8any: 0 environments before, 42 nr vdGs (ATP/NAD CGs) after. The
  same segments are also written as HETATM in the source files (113,720
  standard amino acids on two-char chains; none on one-char chains), so a chain
  remap alone turns protein into "ligands" (8any: 59 TRP-indole CGs). s01 now
  applies both text-level fixes before ProDy parses (`_chain_ids.remap_chain_ids`,
  `_prep_filters.embedded_amino_acid_hetatm_to_atom`), records the remap as
  REMARK 900 lines, and skips structures with > 62 chains (1bos, 8evs).
  `scripts/remap_chain_ids.py` repairs an existing database into a new dir
  (repaired copies need probe re-run). Parsing first would bake the merge in
  silently: ProDy 2.6 gives PHE 4 66 + ASP A4 66 one resindex. Left as HETATM
  after repair (correct): 10 free amino-acid ligands with OXT and no peptide
  bond (6suv GLU 104 x7, 8esw LYS, 7tgh TYR, 8hl4 GLU).
- FIXED IN CODE, NOT YET IN DATA: modified residues without prepwizard (audit
  2026-09-09, `scratch/modres_audit/results/`): genuinely renamed modres = 3322
  residues, 1686 within 4.5 A of a ligand (352 canonical-part contact, 426
  modification-only, 908 both); MSE renamed to MET = 4881 residues, 1148 near a
  ligand in 1137 structures; non-canonical amino acids left in chains = 85
  residues, 15 near a ligand (10 SEC); covalent cofactors (PLP/HEM/HEC/FAD) are
  HETATM and mined as ligands today. s01 now applies
  `_prep_filters.modified_residues_to_protein` before parsing: MSE->MET and
  SEC->CYS (`CYS: {SE}` added to `_ALTERNATE_HEAVY_ATOMS`), then every other
  chain-bonded HETATM residue whose CCD type is `*PEPTIDE LINKING` becomes an
  ATOM record under its own resname (neither slot nor ligand). The CCD type, not
  backbone atom names, is the discriminator: 353 chain-bonded cofactor residues
  (SAM/SAH/NXL/0G6) carry N/CA/C names. Needs `resources/ccd_polymer_types.tsv`
  from `scripts/fetch_ccd_polymer_types.py` (login node; compute nodes have no
  network); without it s01 warns and only the renames happen. Cost: ~1.7k slots
  that are `X` today, all unreachable by hit finding. `X` stays as a safety net;
  no canonical-atoms-within-4.5 A guard needed once nothing is renamed. Verified
  on prepwizard output only (SEC in 5jsy/2wpn/6z9g -> ATOM CYS with SE); the raw
  BioLiP input is on another machine, so the first real exercise of the
  MSE/KCX/LLP paths is the rebuild's s01 run -- spot-check a few outputs.
- Cost-estimate provenance (`estimate_frag_cost`/`make_sge_scripts_for_frags`):
  only `frags_dict_sha256` is checked, not `pdb_dir` — after the 20 A remirror,
  the stale 10 A estimate will still pass and silently drive selection/resourcing.
  Add the check; also make the `--include-only` sha mismatch consistent (currently
  raises) with the general path (currently just warns).

AFTER THE 2026-09-07 VALIDATION RUN (library otherwise sound, zero invariant
violations)
- `_cg_atoms_are_bonded` only checks max nearest-neighbor distance, so it misses
  cases where OpenBabel perceives a bond across a non-bonded (e.g. geminal) pair.
  ~831/2.2M rows affected, 74 fragments. Fix: check SMARTS-bonded pairs directly.
- ~544 rows have deposited-geometry bond lengths too short to be real (modeling
  errors, not a pipeline bug). Decide whether to screen these out.
- FIXED IN CODE, NOT YET IN DATA: `group_protonation_variants` used to collapse
  only onto a charge-free twin that exists in the frags dict, so chemotypes RDKit
  never writes neutral were never pooled: `c[N+;!R](=[O;!R])[O;!R]` and
  `c[N+;!R](=[O;!R])[O-;!R]` were both built in the 2026-09-06 library
  (1243/1230 instances, the second nested in the first). Now >=2 charged
  spellings sharing a charge-stripped form with no twin pool under that form as
  a *promoted* representative (`kind=promoted` in fragment_aliases.tsv;
  `scripts/lookup_fragment_key.py` traces it back). On the production dict this
  is 11 groups: nitro (822 pooled ligands) plus azides, isonitrile, nitrone and
  N-oxide, all below threshold. Before the rebuild: regenerate
  `resources/frag_cost_estimate.tsv` -- `select_fragments` now raises
  "instance_counts is missing 11 fragment(s)" against the old one, by design.
  A lone charged spelling with no twin stays as is (144 such keys; only a
  problem if a second spelling of one appears in a future dict, which the new
  rule then handles).

DESIGN DECISIONS (no urgent deadline)
- Protonation strategy: matching is currently charge-loose (library keys pool
  variants); decide whether to make it charge-strict for donor/acceptor accuracy,
  or keep pooling and rely on the read-path warning for charged-key misses.
- Statistical-support denominator: `identify_bioisosteres/common.py` and
  `hit_finder_core` still use raw cluster/observation counts; `cluster_num_parents`
  (distinct structures) is more correct but unused. Decide which to report.
- Nested fragments (`cccn` inside `cccnc`) share observations, so counts across
  fragments are correlated -- same issue as the overlapping-sites entry in
  pitfalls.md, but systematic once enumeration moves from balls to all connected
  subgraphs. Buckets are independent, so clustering is unaffected; normalize only
  when aggregating across fragments (bioisosteres, hit scoring).
- Calibrate Stage-1 RMSD cutoff `T` against recall on real mined environments (not
  stored nr vdGs). Blocks a real answer on the Stage-2 flanking-context question
  below.
- Stage 2 splits pose clusters by flanking context; unclear if that's a better use
  of representative budget than a tighter `T`. Needs the `T` calibration first.
- Query/library key reachability: build an explicit relaxation map (protonation,
  H-count) instead of ad hoc handling; ring-size differences should stay hard
  constraints (they change CG geometry).
- Aromatic ring-size gate (pyridine/pyrrole, pyrimidine/imidazole currently pool):
  benchmark before adding — unmeasured whether it helps.
- `group_lig_sites_by_overlap` uses single-linkage union-find, which can merge
  genuinely distinct sites on repetitive queries (PEG, polysaccharides). Cheap fix
  (require overlap with every group member) but changes hit counts broadly —
  only do it if drug-like queries stop being the norm.
- Fragment-selection cutoff (`--min-instances 250`) is a judgment call, sensitive
  to sampling noise near the boundary, and ring annotation splits one chemical
  fragment's count across several keys (may be excluding real fragments like
  boronic acids). Revisit before next build.
- Decide how to treat icodes.
- `resolve_fragment_alias`: no longer used by hit finding (only
  `tests/test_fragment_selection.py`). Delete it + its tests, or keep and document.
- `_has_deliberate_charge_assignment`: only fragment that would exercise it is
  below the selection threshold. Delete, or lower the threshold.

WHEN ADDING NEW LIGANDS / REBUILDING THE LIGAND FILE
- `UNDESIRED_ELEMENTS` is a denylist and already leaks (12 CCD elements are
  neither organic nor denylisted); a carbon-bearing complex of one would be mined
  as an organic fragment. Switch to an allowlist (C,N,O,S,P,F,Cl,Br,I,B; decide on
  Se) before adding non-CCD ligands.
- Remove cryoprotectants in `fragment_database_ligs.py`.
- Decide whether hydrogens are needed at all for new structures (depends on
  whether contacts still come from Probe) and if so how to build them.
- Consider a `keys_only` fast path in `fragment_database_ligs.py` that skips the
  permutation/site-grouping machinery (unneeded when only the element string is
  used) — verified no-op on CCD output, worth doing for a rebuild.

OTHER / LOW PRIORITY
- Scrub irrelevant PDBs (PSI/PSII, ribosomes, heavy Fe-S cluster complexes, etc).
- Probe output only needs ligand contacts, not AA-AA; drop the latter to save
  space.
- Add RSCC/RSR/RSRZ to per-vdG quality records (plumbing already exists as a
  no-op).
- Occupancy floor (`min_occ`) is currently inert (prepwizard flattens everything
  to 1.00) — keep it loose; don't filter on it even if a future DB preserves
  occupancy.
- Clustering multiprocessing (`maxtasksperchild=1`) dominates wall time on
  fragments with many small buckets; find out what it's guarding against before
  changing it.
- SGE resource tiers are undersized at the high-automorphism end (phosphates);
  there's also no resume/skip-existing logic, so an h_rt kill discards the whole
  run. Add resume and size tiers on automorphism count, not just structure count.
- CSR storage for the Stage-1 neighbor graph: deferred until a real OOM is
  observed. 2026-09-06 data point: `[O;!R]=[P;!R]([O;!R])[O;!R]` and its 4-O
  sibling were killed by h_rt=28h (not memory) with maxvmem 47.8 GB / 20.0 GB at
  `-pe smp 20`, `mem_free=4G` (per slot, so 80 GB reserved); both died in their
  largest bucket after streaming finished. Rerun needs longer h_rt only.
- CG element-pinning check has a residual gap for unpinned (OR/negation) SMARTS
  atoms; unreachable in production today. Add a pitfalls.md entry if that changes.
- Minor fragment-selection cleanups (`extract_fragment_smiles.py`): silent
  size/solvent-artifact drops with no counter; non-SGE path writes an aliases
  file nothing reads; charge-normalization can silently miss non-first-position
  charges; stale docstring claims/counts; one dead `else` branch.
